Skip field ranges that earlier, overlapping ranges already printed.
cut sorts the -f and -F ranges by their start, and when one range ended
it moved to the next without checking whether that range lay wholly
within the fields already printed. That range then wrote an extra
empty field when the next field was empty or the line had ended, so
"printf 'a\tb\tc\n' | cut -f1-3,2" wrote "a\tb\tc\t" and
"printf 'a\t\tb\n' | cut -f1,1" wrote "a\t". Without -D, pass over
every such range, and every remaining range once the end of the line
has been printed.

---
 coreutils/cut.c |    3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/cut.c
+++ b/coreutils/cut.c
@@ -199,6 +199,9 @@
 						break;
 					if (option_mask32 & OPT_NOSORT)
 						start = dcount = next = 0;
+					else if (end == linelen || dcount > cut_list[cl_pos].endpos)
+						/* sorted ranges: this one is already printed */
+						goto end_of_range;
 					end = 0; /* (why?) */
 					//bb_error_msg("End of current range");
 				}
